# Line endings are the repository's to decide, not each machine's.
#
# Every text file here is stored and checked out with LF. Without this line that depended on each
# contributor having `core.autocrlf=input` set, which is a setting on a machine rather than a
# property of the repository: a clone on a default Windows install would check out CRLF, and the
# first commit from it would rewrite whole files as line ending changes.
#
# `text=auto` rather than `text`, so Git still decides per file. One test file carries NUL bytes on
# purpose and is detected as binary; forcing every path to text would rewrite it.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Vendored normative sources are byte-exact, and Git must not touch them.
#
# `standards/sources.lock.json` records a SHA-256 of every file under `standards/data/`, and seven
# of the XLIFF 2.2 schemas are published with CRLF endings. This repository runs with
# `core.autocrlf=input`, so without the line below those seven would be normalized to LF on the way
# into the object store: the working copy that recorded the digests would go on verifying, and
# every fresh clone would fail. A provenance record that only holds on the machine that wrote it is
# not a provenance record.
#
# `-text` rather than `binary`, so the files still diff as text when one of them is replaced.
standards/data/** -text
